claimed: intent-distil-for-probe — the learning curve said the probe is flat past 32 examples and I concluded that labels are not its constraint. That conclusion was drawn on MY sentences only: 120 messages by one author in one register. The distilled corpus has a measurably different distribution -- trained on it alone chargrams score ten points BELOW a human fixture a third the size -- so the flatness may be about homogeneity rather than quantity, and the cheap test is to embed the 182 distilled messages and refit the probe on fixture+distilled. Pays under either answer to the open question of whether a deployment can host an encoder: if it can, this is the lever on the 88.3%; if it cannot, it is evidence about what the no-network tiers need. Evaluation stays on held-out HUMAN data, as in the distillation lane, so a corpus cannot score itself.
